            {
              "id": "glog-8da302eb-4c60-490b-aa7e-f026f9bfbb07",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-29577** affects the `tensorflow` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, available on PyPI. This vulnerability arises from a flaw in the implementation of the `SparseFillEmptyRows` operation in TensorFlow. The issue is due to a missing validation check on the input arguments, which can lead to a heap buffer overflow. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service (DoS) by crashing the application.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade TensorFlow to a version where this vulnerability is patched. Check the TensorFlow release notes for the version where this issue is resolved and update accordingly.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement additional input validation checks in your application to ensure that inputs to TensorFlow operations are within expected bounds.\n\n3. **Security Patches**: Regularly apply security patches and updates to your dependencies to protect against known vulnerabilities.\n\n4. **Code Review**: Conduct thorough code reviews and static analysis to identify and mitigate potential vulnerabilities in your codebase.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe fix for this vulnerability involves adding proper validation checks for the input arguments to the `SparseFillEmptyRows` operation. Ensure that the input tensors are validated for size and type before processing. Here is a conceptual example of how you might add such checks:\n\n```python\nimport tensorflow as tf\n\ndef safe_sparse_fill_empty_rows(indices, values, dense_shape):\n    # Validate input sizes\n    if not indices.shape[0] == values.shape[0]:\n        raise ValueError(\"Indices and values must have the same number of rows.\")\n    \n    # Validate dense_shape\n    if len(dense_shape) != 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Dense shape must be a 2D tensor.\")\n\n    # Proceed with the operation if inputs are valid\n    return tf.raw_ops.SparseFillEmptyRows(\n        indices=indices,\n        values=values,\n        dense_shape=dense_shape\n    )\n```\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-29577/pkg:pypi/tensorflow@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8838502c-21d7-405b-bc17-4c4c6092b954",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in the C++ code arises from the improper use of the `memcpy` function. The line in question:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy((*htblptr)->bits, bits, sizeof((*htblptr));\n```\n\nThis line attempts to copy memory from `bits` to `(*htblptr)->bits` using the size of `(*htblptr)`. The issue here is that `sizeof((*htblptr))` likely does not represent the correct size of the destination buffer `(*htblptr)->bits`. This can lead to buffer overflows, which may result in undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Buffer Sizes**: Ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Functions**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `memmove` if overlapping memory regions are possible.\n3. **Boundary Checks**: Always perform boundary checks before copying data to prevent overflows.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the vulnerability, ensure that the correct size is used in the `memcpy` function. Assuming `(*htblptr)->bits` is an array, the size should be the size of the array, not the size of the pointer. Here's a corrected version:\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming 'bits' is an array of the same type and size as '(*htblptr)->bits'\nmemcpy((*htblptr)->bits, bits, sizeof((*htblptr)->bits));\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard library headers are typically required:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>  // For memcpy\n```\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
