                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-29604** affects the `tensorflow-gpu` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, which is a part of the TensorFlow library used for machine learning tasks. This vulnerability arises from an issue in the implementation of certain operations that can lead to a denial of service (DoS) or potentially allow for arbitrary code execution. The root cause is typically improper validation of input data, which can lead to buffer overflows or other memory corruption issues.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability has been patched. Check the TensorFlow GitHub repository or PyPI for the latest secure version.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are within expected bounds and types.\n\n3. **Use Virtual Environments**: Run TensorFlow within a virtual environment to limit the impact of any potential exploit.\n\n4. **Regular Security Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and mitigate potential vulnerabilities.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability would be found in the TensorFlow repository, a general approach to fixing such vulnerabilities includes:\n\n- **Bounds Checking**: Ensure that all buffer accesses are within bounds.\n- **Sanitize Inputs**: Validate and sanitize all inputs to TensorFlow operations to prevent unexpected data from causing memory corruption.\n- **Use Safe Libraries**: Where possible, use libraries and functions that provide built-in safety checks.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- **CWE-20: Improper Input Validation**: This vulnerability is closely related to improper input validation, which can lead to various security issues, including buffer overflows and code execution.\n\nFor more detailed information on CWE-20, you can visit the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)."
