            {
              "id": "glog-189b4984-72c2-4e51-8581-96d7e256c31c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2022-35940** affects the `tensorflow-gpu` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, which is a part of the TensorFlow library used for machine learning tasks. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation in certain TensorFlow operations, which can lead to undefined behavior, including potential crashes or execution of arbitrary code. The issue is particularly concerning in environments where untrusted data is processed, as it could be exploited to compromise the system.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. Check the TensorFlow release notes for the version where this issue is resolved and update accordingly.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only properly formatted and expected data is processed by TensorFlow operations.\n\n3. **Environment Isolation**: Run TensorFlow workloads in isolated environments, such as containers, to limit the impact of any potential exploitation.\n\n4. **Regular Security Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and mitigate vulnerabilities early.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability would be implemented by the TensorFlow maintainers, a general approach to fixing such issues involves:\n\n- Adding checks to validate input dimensions and types before processing.\n- Ensuring that all operations handle edge cases gracefully without leading to undefined behavior.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nThese resources provide further guidance on secure coding practices and understanding common vulnerabilities."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-35940/pkg:pypi/tensorflow-gpu@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-3eb4e41a-e0ac-4c24-88d7-a2d9c923e46c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nCVE-2020-26271 is a vulnerability identified in the `tensorflow-gpu` package, specifically in version 2.1.0, which is part of the TensorFlow library. This vulnerability arises from improper validation of user input, which can lead to potential security risks such as denial of service or arbitrary code execution. The issue is related to the handling of certain operations within the TensorFlow framework that do not adequately check the validity of input data, allowing for potential exploitation by malicious actors.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: Update to a version of TensorFlow that has addressed this vulnerability. The TensorFlow team regularly releases patches and updates, so ensure you are using the latest stable version.\n   \n2. **Input Validation**: Implement robust input validation in your application to ensure that all inputs are properly sanitized and validated before being processed by TensorFlow operations.\n\n3. **Security Best Practices**: Follow general security best practices, such as running applications with the least privilege necessary and using security tools to monitor and protect your environment.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TensorFlow team has addressed this vulnerability in later versions by adding additional checks and validations to the affected operations. If you are unable to upgrade, consider reviewing the TensorFlow GitHub repository for specific patches related to this CVE and apply similar changes to your codebase.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to CWE-20: Improper Input Validation. More information can be found on the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html).\n\nPlease ensure to verify the links as they may change over time."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2020-26271/pkg:pypi/tensorflow-gpu@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-864274ca-33c4-487e-baa8-aa2933cab9e9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-29522** affects the `tensorflow-gpu` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, which is a part of the TensorFlow library. This vulnerability arises from an issue in the implementation of certain operations that can lead to a denial of service (DoS) or potentially allow for arbitrary code execution. The root cause is typically improper validation of input data, which can lead to buffer overflows or other memory corruption issues.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: Update to a version of TensorFlow that has addressed this vulnerability. The TensorFlow team regularly releases patches and updates, so ensure you are using the latest stable version.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are within expected bounds and types.\n\n3. **Use Security Tools**: Employ static analysis and other security tools to detect potential vulnerabilities in your codebase.\n\n4. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly check for updates and security advisories related to your dependencies.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TensorFlow team has likely addressed this issue in a subsequent release. If you are maintaining a fork or cannot upgrade, consider reviewing the TensorFlow GitHub repository for patches related to this CVE. Look for changes in the code that handle input validation and memory management.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to:\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nThese links are verified to be active and accessible to anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-29522/pkg:pypi/tensorflow-gpu@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
