            {
              "id": "glog-41cb497a-a63b-4e33-b254-765f5c2edeff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**CVE-2017-3526** is a vulnerability in the Java SE component of Oracle Java SE (subcomponent: Libraries). It is a high-severity issue that affects versions prior to 7u131, 8u121, and 9. The vulnerability allows unauthenticated attackers to exploit the flaw via a crafted request, potentially leading to unauthorized access to sensitive data or a denial of service.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Update Java SE**: Ensure that your Java SE installation is updated to a version that is not affected by this vulnerability. Specifically, upgrade to Java SE 7u131, 8u121, 9, or later.\r\n\r\n2. **Apply Security Patches**: Regularly apply security patches provided by Oracle to mitigate potential vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n3. **Limit Network Exposure**: Restrict network exposure for all control systems and devices. Ensure that they are not accessible from the internet unless absolutely necessary.\r\n\r\n4. **Use Firewalls**: Implement firewalls to block unauthorized access to vulnerable systems.\r\n\r\n5. **Monitor and Audit**: Regularly monitor and audit systems for unusual activity that may indicate an attempted exploitation of the vulnerability.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability is not publicly available, the general recommendation is to:\r\n\r\n- Review and update any custom Java libraries or applications that may rely on vulnerable components.\r\n- Refactor code to use updated and secure libraries.\r\n- Conduct thorough testing to ensure that updates do not introduce new vulnerabilities or break existing functionality.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- **CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data**: This vulnerability is related to the deserialization of untrusted data, which can lead to remote code execution or other security issues. More information can be found on the [CWE-502 page](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2017-3526/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-3cd0d495-9a2d-4071-96e9-b5808cfcd7a9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**CVE-2017-3514** is a vulnerability found in the Java SE component of Oracle Java SE (subcomponent: Libraries). It affects versions prior to 7u131, 8u121, and 9. This vulnerability allows an unauthenticated attacker to cause a denial of service (DoS) by leveraging the improper handling of certain inputs. The issue is related to the deserialization of untrusted data, which can lead to application crashes or other unintended behaviors.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Update Java SE**: Ensure that you are using the latest version of Java SE. Oracle has released patches for this vulnerability, so upgrading to a version that is 7u131, 8u121, 9, or later will mitigate the issue.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only trusted data is deserialized. Avoid deserializing data from untrusted sources.\r\n\r\n3. **Use Security Manager**: Consider using Java's Security Manager to enforce a security policy that restricts the actions that can be performed by untrusted code.\r\n\r\n4. **Deserialization Filters**: Use deserialization filters to control what classes can be deserialized. This can prevent the deserialization of potentially harmful classes.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability in your code, you can implement a deserialization filter. Here is an example of how to set a global deserialization filter in Java:\r\n\r\n```java\r\nimport java.io.ObjectInputFilter;\r\n\r\npublic class DeserializationFilterExample {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        ObjectInputFilter filter = ObjectInputFilter.Config.createFilter(\"maxdepth=5;!*\");\r\n        ObjectInputFilter.Config.setSerialFilter(filter);\r\n\r\n        // Your deserialization logic here\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis filter limits the depth of the object graph to 5 and blocks all other classes (`!*`). Adjust the filter rules according to your application's needs.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Deserialization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten 2021: A08:2021 - Software and Data Integrity Failures](https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2017-3514/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ba9d118a-ca17-49d4-b51b-e8fdcb2b4809",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**CVE-2017-3533** is a vulnerability found in the Java SE component of Oracle Java SE (subcomponent: Libraries). It affects versions prior to 8u131. The vulnerability allows an unauthenticated attacker to cause a denial of service (DoS) by leveraging the improper handling of certain input data. This issue is related to the improper validation of input data, which can lead to unexpected application behavior or crashes.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: The primary mitigation strategy is to upgrade to a version of Java SE that is not affected by this vulnerability. Specifically, upgrading to Java SE 8u131 or later will resolve this issue.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement robust input validation to ensure that all input data is properly sanitized and validated before processing. This can help prevent unexpected behavior caused by malformed input.\r\n\r\n3. **Security Patches**: Regularly apply security patches and updates to your Java environment to protect against known vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n4. **Monitoring and Logging**: Implement monitoring and logging to detect and respond to unusual application behavior that may indicate an attempted exploitation of this vulnerability.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nWhile the primary recommendation is to upgrade to a secure version of Java, developers should also ensure that their code properly validates and sanitizes input data. Here is a general example of how to implement input validation in Java:\r\n\r\n```java\r\nimport java.util.regex.Pattern;\r\n\r\npublic class InputValidator {\r\n    private static final Pattern VALID_INPUT_PATTERN = Pattern.compile(\"^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$\");\r\n\r\n    public static boolean isValidInput(String input) {\r\n        if (input == null) {\r\n            return false;\r\n        }\r\n        return VALID_INPUT_PATTERN.matcher(input).matches();\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        String userInput = \"exampleInput123\";\r\n        if (isValidInput(userInput)) {\r\n            System.out.println(\"Valid input.\");\r\n        } else {\r\n            System.out.println(\"Invalid input.\");\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2017-3533/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ffd78e48-19c6-451f-800d-e3d2322f4277",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\nCVE-2017-3539 is a vulnerability in the Java SE component of Oracle Java SE (subcomponent: Libraries). It affects versions prior to 7u131, 8u121, and 9. This vulnerability allows an unauthenticated attacker to cause a denial of service (DoS) through a crafted HTTP request. The issue is due to improper handling of certain HTTP requests, which can lead to resource exhaustion.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade Java SE**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a non-vulnerable version of Java SE. Ensure you are using version 7u131, 8u121, 9, or later.\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only well-formed HTTP requests are processed.\r\n3. **Rate Limiting**: Apply rate limiting on incoming requests to prevent resource exhaustion from excessive requests.\r\n4. **Monitoring and Logging**: Enable detailed logging and monitoring to detect and respond to unusual patterns of requests that may indicate an attack.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability is not publicly available, general recommendations include:\r\n\r\n- Review and update the HTTP request handling logic to ensure it can gracefully handle unexpected or malformed requests.\r\n- Implement robust error handling to prevent the application from crashing or becoming unresponsive due to unexpected input.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption ('Resource Exhaustion')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2017-3539/suse:java:"
                ]
              }
            },
